L000390	\section{Possible magnetic multipoles in antiferroaxial altermagnetism}\label{sec:1}
L000391	
L000392	In this section, we classify the magnetic multipoles induced by coexisting antiferroaxial and N\'{e}el orders. This classification determines the symmetry allowed antiferroaxial altermagnetic spin splitting types in each crystallographic point group.
L000393	We work in the limit of zero spin-orbit coupling (SOC), where the spin and real-space sectors are separable.
L000394	
L000395	In the parent space group $\mathcal G_0$, an antiferroaxial order parameter $\mathbf G$ transforms as
L000396	$\mathbf G \sim \Gamma_G(\mathbf q)\otimes \Gamma_A$,
L000397	where $\Gamma_G(\mathbf q)$ is an irreducible representation (IR) of the little group at propagation vector $\mathbf q$ that encodes the staggered spatial modulation, and $\Gamma_A$ denotes the axial-vector representation of the crystallographic point group.
L000398	Similarly, the N\'{e}el vector transforms as
L000399	$\mathbf N \sim \Gamma_N(\mathbf q')\otimes \Gamma_A^{s}$,
L000400	where $\Gamma_N(\mathbf q')$ is an IR of the little group at $\mathbf q'$ and $\Gamma_A^{s}$ is the axial-vector representation in spin-rotation space.
L000401	
L000402	The nonrelativistic spin splitting in altermagnets can be parameterized by magnetic multipoles, defined as rank-$\ell$ symmetric-traceless moments of the magnetization density $\mathbf m(\mathbf r)$,
L000403	\begin{equation}
L000404	\mathbf O^{(\ell)} \equiv \int d^3r \,\big[r_{\mu_1}r_{\mu_2}\cdots r_{\mu_\ell}\big]\mathbf m(\mathbf r),
L000405	\label{eq:Omultipole_SM}
L000406	\end{equation}
L000407	where $r_\mu$ are Cartesian components of $\mathbf r$ and $[\cdots]$ denotes the symmetric-traceless projection over $\mu_1\cdots\mu_\ell$.
L000408	By construction, $\mathbf O^{(\ell)}$ transforms as $\Gamma_\ell\otimes \Gamma_A^{s}$, where $\Gamma_\ell$ is the spatial rank-$\ell$ representation (dimension $2\ell+1$) that subduces into IRs of the crystallographic point group.
L000409	The ranks $\ell=2,4,6$ correspond to the $d$-, $g$-, and $i$-wave altermagnetic symmetries, respectively.
L000410	
L000411	We consider a symmetry-allowed trilinear invariant coupling among $\mathbf G$, $\mathbf N$, and $\mathbf O^{(\ell)}$. In the parent phase, the identity representation must preserve the translational symmetry of the parent group, which enforces momentum conservation, $\mathbf q + \mathbf q' = \mathbf K$ (where $\mathbf K$ is a reciprocal lattice vector); i.e., $\mathbf q' = -\mathbf q$ up to a reciprocal lattice vector.
L000412	Since $\Gamma_1^{s}\subset \Gamma_A^{s}\otimes\Gamma_A^{s}$, the invariance condition reduces to the real-space sector
L000413	\begin{equation}
L000414	\Gamma_1 \subset \Gamma_G(\mathbf q)\otimes \Gamma_A \otimes \Gamma_N(\mathbf q') \otimes \Gamma_\ell .
L000415	\label{eq:tri_invariant_SM}
L000416	\end{equation}
L000417	Hereafter, we suppress the spin-rotation factor $\Gamma_A^s$ for notational brevity. It is convenient to introduce the combined modulation representation
L000418	$\Gamma_0 \equiv \Gamma_G(\mathbf q)\otimes \Gamma_N(\mathbf q')$,
L000419	so that the bilinear coupling transforms as $\mathbf N \mathbf G\sim \Gamma_0\otimes \Gamma_A$.
L000420	Equation~(\ref{eq:tri_invariant_SM}) then reduces to a purely spatial selection rule: a trilinear invariant exists if and only if $\Gamma_0\otimes\Gamma_A$ and the multipole sector $\Gamma_\ell$ share at least one common point-group IR, i.e.,
L000421	\begin{equation}
L000422	\big(\Gamma_0\otimes\Gamma_A\big)\cap \Gamma_\ell \neq \emptyset .
L000423	\end{equation}
L000424	Therefore, once $\Gamma_G(\mathbf q)$ and $\Gamma_N(\mathbf q')$ are specified in the parent group, this criterion fixes the lowest allowed multipole rank $\ell_{\min}$ (and hence the $d$/$g$/$i$-wave class), while the detailed basis form (e.g., $d_{xy}$ versus $d_{x^2-y^2}$) is only resolved after the order parameter condenses and the system reduces to the isotropy subgroup.
L000425	
L000426	In practice, for each crystallographic point group, we:
L000427	(i) specify the real-space axial-vector representation $\Gamma_A$;
L000428	(ii) enumerate admissible $\Gamma_0$ compatible with a collinear N\'{e}el order in that point group (for centrosymmetric groups, $\Gamma_0$ must be inversion-even for the even-$\ell$ multipoles considered here);
L000429	(iii) tabulate $\Gamma_0\otimes\Gamma_A$; and
L000430	(iv) determine the lowest $\ell=2,4,6$ whose multipole decomposition contains at least one IR that also appears in the decomposition of $\Gamma_0\otimes\Gamma_A$.
L000431	The decompositions of $\Gamma_\ell$ into point-group IRs are listed in Table~\ref{table:TB1}, and the resulting allowed spin-splitting types are summarized in Table~\ref{table:TB2}.
L000432	
L000433	This analysis shows that antiferroaxiality generically enables $d$-, $g$-, and $i$-wave altermagnetic spin splitting depending on the point-group symmetry and the specific $\Gamma_0$ channel selected by the antiferroaxial--N\'{e}el coexistence.
