L000771	\subsection{$d$-wave antiferroaxial altermagnetic tight-binding model}
L000772	
L000773	We construct the $d$-wave model based on the parent space group $P4/mmm$, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:TB}(a1). Both the antiferromagnetic and antiferroaxial instabilities occur at the $M$ point with wavevector $\mathbf{q}=(\pi,\pi,0)$, transforming as $\mathbf{N}\sim M_1^+$ and $\mathbf{G}\sim M_4^+\otimes(\Gamma_2^+\oplus\Gamma_5^+)$, respectively. Here, $M_4^+$ is the representation of the staggered pattern for the antiferroaxial order, which implies that the clockwise and counterclockwise rotational units are exchanged under $C_{4z}$ and $m_x$, but remain invariant under $m_{110}$ [the operators are plotted in Fig.~\ref{fig:TB}(b1)]. For the N\'{e}el vector, the spin-rotation space representation $\Gamma_A^s$ is suppressed, as the condition $\Gamma_1^s\subset \Gamma_A^s\otimes\Gamma_A^s$ is always satisfied. The ferroaxial order $\Gamma_A$ in the point group $4/mmm$ (corresponding to the space group $P4/mmm$) decomposes into the irreducible representation $\Gamma_2^+ \oplus \Gamma_5^+$. For the case with $\mathbf{G}\parallel \hat{z}$, we take $G_z \sim M_4^+\otimes\Gamma_2^+ = M_3^+$. The bilinear combination then transforms as
L000774	\begin{equation}
L000775	\mathbf{N}G_z\sim M_1^+\otimes M_3^+=\Gamma_3^+(B_{1g}),
L000776	\end{equation}
L000777	which enables a symmetry-allowed trilinear invariant with the rank-2 magnetic multipole sector in the parent group. Upon condensation of $G_z$, the symmetry is reduced to $P4/mbm$ with crystallographic point group $4/mmm$. In this subgroup, the N\'{e}el vector, which transforms as $M_1^+$ in the parent phase, subduces to the one-dimensional irrep $B_{2g}$. By consulting Table~\ref{table:altermagnet_valid_only}, we identify $B_{2g}$ with a $d_{xy}$-wave altermagnetic spin splitting and the corresponding spin point group $^{\bar{1}}4/m^{\bar{1}}mm$.
L000778	
L000779	In the TB model, we include nearest-neighbor hopping between magnetic atoms ($t_M$) and between ligand atoms ($t$). For magnetic-ligand hybridization, we include both nearest- and next-nearest-neighbor hoppings ($V_1$ and $V_2$), as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:TB}(a2), which can be modulated by the antiferroaxial distortion angle $\theta$ through Harrison's rule~\cite{PhysRevB.20.2420}
L000780	\begin{equation}
L000781	\begin{split}
L000782	V_1(\theta) &= V_1^0\frac{|\mathbf{r}(0)|^2}{|\mathbf{r}(\theta)|^2}
L000783	= V_1^0\frac{\sin^2(\pi/4)}{\sin^2(\pi/4-\theta)}
L000784	= \frac{V_1^0}{1-\sin(2\theta)},\\
L000785	V_2(\theta) &= V_2^0\frac{|\mathbf{r}(0)|^2}{|\mathbf{r}(\theta)|^2}
L000786	= V_2^0\frac{\sin^2(\pi/4)}{\sin^2(\pi/4+\theta)}
L000787	= \frac{V_2^0}{1+\sin(2\theta)},
L000788	\end{split}
L000789	\end{equation}
L000790	where we assume the ligand-ligand bond length remains constant so that $t$ is independent of $\theta$. The factor $\sin(\pi/4)$ and $\sin(\pi/4\pm\theta)$ are the hopping distance parameters before and after the distortion, respectively.
L000791	
L000792	Reversing the antiferroaxial order, $\theta\to-\theta$, yields the structure in Fig.~\ref{fig:TB}(a3).
L000793	This configuration is symmetry-equivalent to applying $[E||C_{4z}]$ to Fig.~\ref{fig:TB}(a2), where $E$ denotes the identity in spin space and $C_{4z}$ represents a four-fold rotation in real space.
L000794	As a result, the spin splitting reverses sign, consistent with the reversal of the corresponding magnetic multipole component (cf.~Fig.~2(a3) in the main text).
L000795	
L000796	\subsection{$g$-wave antiferroaxial altermagnetic tight-binding model}
L000797	
L000798	For the planar $g$-wave antiferroaxial altermagnetic model, we consider the tetragonal parent space group $P4/mmm$, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:TB}(b1), where green dashed lines indicate the $m_x$ and $m_{110}$ mirror planes. In contrast to the $d$-wave case, the antiferromagnetic and antiferroaxial instabilities here share the same spatial modulation at the $M$ point, i.e., $\Gamma_N(\mathbf{q}')=\Gamma_G(\mathbf{q})=M_1^+$ with $\mathbf{q}=\mathbf{q}'=(\pi,\pi,0)$. Consequently, we have $G_z\sim M_1^+\otimes\Gamma_2^+=M_2^+$ and $\mathbf{N}\sim M_1^+$. The bilinear combination transforms as
L000799	\begin{equation}
L000800	\mathbf{N} G_z \sim M_1^+\otimes M_2^+ = \Gamma_2^+(A_{2g}),
L000801	\end{equation}
L000802	which allows coupling to the $A_{2g}$ channel of the rank-4 magnetic multipole $\mathbf{O}^{(4)}$ (see Table~\ref{table:TB1}). Upon condensation of $G_z$, the symmetry is lowered to the isotropy subgroup $P4/mbm$; notably, the crystallographic point group remains $4/mmm$. In this subgroup, the N\'{e}el order parameter (originally $M_1^+$) subduces to the $A_{2g}$ irreducible representation. As listed in Table~\ref{table:altermagnet_valid_only}, this representation corresponds to a planar $g$-wave altermagnetic spin splitting characterized by the spin point group $4/m^{\bar{1}}m^{\bar{1}}m$.
L000803	
L000804	
L000805	The Hamiltonian follows Eq.~(\ref{eq:hamiltonian}), with hopping paths shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:TB}(b2).
L000806	The antiferroaxial rotation angle $\theta$ modulates the ligand-ligand hoppings $t_1$ and $t_2$, while the base hoppings' amplitude $t$ and $V_0$ remain invariant. Their functional forms are given by
L000807	\begin{equation}
L000808	\begin{split}
L000809	t_1(\theta) &= t_1^0
L000810	\frac{\sin^2(\pi/4)}{\sin^2(\pi/4-\theta)}
L000811	= \frac{t_1^0}{1-\sin(2\theta)},\\
L000812	t_2(\theta) &= t_2^0
L000813	\frac{\sin^2(\pi/4)}{\sin^2(\pi/4+\theta)}
L000814	= \frac{t_2^0}{1+\sin(2\theta)},
L000815	\end{split}
L000816	\end{equation}
L000817	where the factors $\sin(\pi/4)$ and $\sin(\pi/4\pm\theta)$ in the numerators correspond to the hopping distance parameters before and after the distortion, respectively.
L000818	
L000819	Reversing the antiferroaxial order, $\theta\to-\theta$, gives the structure in Fig.~\ref{fig:TB}(b3).
L000820	This configuration is symmetry-equivalent to applying $[C_2||\boldsymbol{\tau}]$ to Fig.~\ref{fig:TB}(b2), where $C_2$ denotes a $\pi$ rotation in spin space about an axis perpendicular to the ordered moments and $\boldsymbol{\tau}=(1/2,1/2,0)$ is a real-space translation.
L000821	As a result, the spin splitting reverses sign, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:TB}(b4) (cf.~Fig.~2(b3) in the main text).
L000822	
L000823	
L000824	\subsection{$i$-wave antiferroaxial altermagnetic tight-binding model}
L000825	
L000826	
L000827	For the planar $i$-wave antiferroaxial altermagnetic model, we adopt the hexagonal parent space group $P6/mmm$, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:TB}(c1), where the green dashed lines indicate the $m_{110}$ and $m_{\bar{1}10}$ mirror planes. In this hexagonal geometry, both the antiferromagnetic and antiferroaxial instabilities occur at the $\Gamma$ point. The staggered spatial modulation, which is shared by the N\'{e}el and antiferroaxial patterns, transforms as the one-dimensional representation $\Gamma_3^-$. Consequently, the N\'{e}el vector transforms as $\mathbf{N}\sim \Gamma_3^-$. The antiferroaxial order parameter $G_z$ is constructed by combining this staggered modulation with the axial-vector representation $\Gamma_2^+(A_{2g})$ of the $6/mmm$ point group, yielding $G_z \sim \Gamma_3^- \otimes \Gamma_2^+ = \Gamma_4^-$. The resulting bilinear combination transforms as
L000828	\begin{equation}
L000829	\mathbf{N}G_z \sim \Gamma_3^- \otimes \Gamma_4^- = \Gamma_2^+(A_{2g}).
L000830	\end{equation}
L000831	This coupling enables a symmetry-allowed trilinear invariant with the $A_{2g}$ channel of the rank-6 magnetic multipole $\mathbf{O}^{(6)}$ (see Table~\ref{table:TB1}). Upon condensation of $G_z$, the symmetry is reduced to the isotropy subgroup $P\bar{6}m2$. In this subgroup, the N\'{e}el vector (originally $\Gamma_3^-$) subduces to the representation $A_2'$. As listed in Table~\ref{table:altermagnet_valid_only}, this representation corresponds to a planar $i$-wave altermagnetic spin splitting.
L000832	
L000833	The tight-binding Hamiltonian includes the nearest-neighbor hopping between magnetic atoms ($t_M$), ligand-ligand hopping ($t$), and magnetic-ligand hybridization ($V$). The antiferroaxial rotation $\theta$ lifts the symmetry of the ligand environment, resulting in two inequivalent ligand-ligand hoppings ($t_1, t_2$) and two inequivalent magnetic-ligand hybridizations ($V_1, V_2$), as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:TB}(c2). Incorporating the structural dependence via Harrison's scaling law, the hopping amplitudes are given by
L000834	\begin{equation}
L000835	\begin{split}
L000836	t_{1}(\theta) &= t_{1}^0\frac{(\sqrt{3})^2}{2-2\cos(2\pi/3+ 2\theta)}= \frac{3t_{1}^0}{2\left[1+\cos(2\theta - \pi/3)\right]},\\
L000837	t_{2}(\theta) &= t_{2}^0\frac{(\sqrt{3})^2}{2-2\cos(2\pi/3- 2\theta)}= \frac{3t_{2}^0}{2\left[1+\cos(2\theta + \pi/3)\right]},\\
L000838	V_{1}(\theta) &= V_{1}^0\frac{7/3}{4/3\cos^2\theta+1/3-4/3\cos\theta\cos(2\pi/3+\theta)} =\frac{7V_{1}^0}{4 + 2\sqrt{3}\sin(2\theta + \pi/3)},\\
L000839	V_{2}(\theta) &= V_{2}^0\frac{7/3}{4/3\cos^2\theta+1/3-4/3\cos\theta\cos(2\pi/3-\theta)} =\frac{7V_{2}^0}{4 - 2\sqrt{3}\sin(2\theta - \pi/3)},
L000840	\end{split}
L000841	\end{equation}
L000842	where the denominators reflect the changes in bond lengths induced by the distortion angle $\theta$.
L000843	
L000844	
L000845	Reversing the antiferroaxial order, $\theta\to-\theta$, yields the structure in Fig.~\ref{fig:TB}(c3).
L000846	This configuration is related to Fig.~\ref{fig:TB}(c2) by the symmetry operation $[E\Vert m_{\bar{1}10}]$, with $E$ the identity in spin space.
L000847	Accordingly, the induced $\Gamma_2^+(A_{2g})$ multipole and the associated planar $i$-wave altermagnetic spin splitting change sign under $\theta\to-\theta$, consistent with the reversal shown in the main text [Fig.~2(c3)].
L000848	
