L001112	\section{Computational details and candidate antiferroaxial altermagnets}\label{sec:material}
L001113	
L001114	\subsection{First-principles calculations and Wannier-based tight-binding models}
L001115	The first-principles calculations of structure optimization and band structures are using Vienna \textit{ab initio} simulation package(VASP)\cite{kresseEfficientIterativeSchemes1996}. The generalized gradient approximation (GGA) with Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE)\cite{perdewGeneralizedGradientApproximation1996a} realization is considered. For correlated $3d$ orbitals, a Hubbard $U$ correction was employed as specified for each material below.
L001116	
L001117	To evaluate Berry-curvature-related responses (e.g., anomalous Hall conductivity in FeF$_3$), we constructed \textit{ab initio} tight-binding Hamiltonians based on maximally localized Wannier functions using Wannier90 code~\cite{mostofiWannier90ToolObtaining2008}. Unless otherwise stated, the nonrelativistic altermagnetic spin splitting discussed in this work is analyzed in the zero SOC limit. In this section, we suppress the axial-vector representation $\Gamma_A^s$ in spin-rotational space.
L001118	
L001119	In this section, we provide the computational details, symmetry analyses, and calculated physical properties for all candidate antiferroaxial altermagnets identified in this work, including MnS$_2$, MnC$_2$, FeBr$_3$, KMnF$_3$, Ca$_2$MnO$_4$, FeF$_3$, CoF$_3$, LaCrO$_3$, and La$_2$NiO$_4$.
L001120	
L001121	
L001122	
L001123	\subsection{2D pentagonal planar $g$-wave altermagnets: MnA$_2$ (A = S, C)}
L001124	\label{subsec:MnA2}
L001125	
L001126	Figure~\ref{fig:MnS2} summarizes the crystal structures, magnetic configurations, and electronic band structures of the 2D pentagonal systems MnS$_2$ and MnC$_2$~\cite{wangPentagonal2DAltermagnetsb}. The parent phase belongs to the space group $P\bar{4}m2$, where the primitive cell contains a magnetic Mn atom coordinated by nonmagnetic ligands, forming a M-A$_4$ (A=S, C) tetrahedral unit with $S_{4z}$ symmetry. Both the antiferromagnetic and antiferroaxial instabilities occur at the $M$ point $\mathbf{q}=(\pi,\pi,0)$, yielding a C-type antiferromagnetic order. In the zero SOC limit, the N\'{e}el vector transforms as $\mathbf{N}\sim M_1$, and the antiferroaxial order corresponds to the counter-rotation of neighboring ligand tetrahedra around the $z$ axis, characterized by a staggered axial vector $G_z$ transforming as $G_z\sim M_2$. Consequently, their bilinear product transforms as a $\Gamma$-point representation,
L001127	\begin{equation}
L001128	G_z\mathbf{N}\sim M_1\otimes M_2=\Gamma_2(A_2),
L001129	\end{equation}
L001130	and thus couples to the $A_2$ component of the rank four multipoles,
L001131	\begin{equation}
L001132	\mathbf{O}^{(4)}\sim 2A_1\oplus A_2\oplus B_1\oplus B_2\oplus 2E.
L001133	\end{equation}
L001134	The isotropy subgroup is $P\bar{4}2_1m$, in which the subduced N\'{e}el order transforms as $\Gamma_2(A_2)$, resulting in the planar $g$-wave altermagnetic spin splitting according to Table~\ref{table:altermagnet_valid_only}, consistent with DFT results shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:MnS2}(c,f).
L001135	
L001136	\textit{Computational details:} For MnS$_2$ and MnC$_2$, we used $U=4$ eV on Mn $3d$ orbitals. The Brillouin zone was sampled using a $13\times 13\times 1$ k mesh, and the plane-wave cutoff energy was set to 550 eV.
