L001172	\subsection{3D planar $g$-wave altermagnets: KMnF$_3$ and Ca$_2$MnO$_4$}
L001173	\label{subsec:KMnF3_Ca2MnO4}
L001174	
L001175	KMnF$_3$~\cite{knightNuclearMagneticStructures2020} is a representative perovskite-based planar $g$-wave altermagnet. The antiferroaxial order is realized by a staggered rotation of corner-sharing MnF$_6$ octahedra, which we parameterize by the rotation angle $\theta$, and the magnetic ground state is the $G$-type antiferromagnet [Fig.~\ref{fig:KMnF3}(a)]. Within the Landau description in Sec.~\ref{sec:3}, the planar character is selected when the antiferroaxial anisotropy favors a principal-axis configuration, e.g., $\mathbf G \parallel \hat z$, which yields the planar $g$-wave spin-splitting form.
L001176	
L001177	Starting from the cubic parent space group $Pm\bar{3}m$, both the antiferroaxial distortion and the N\'{e}el order are associated with the $R$ point. Their condensation lowers the symmetry to the isotropy subgroup $I4/mcm$, leading to a planar $g$-wave altermagnetic spin splitting in the $xy(x^2-y^2)$ channel. The energetic barrier of the antiferroaxial distortion is quantified by the DFT total-energy profile as a function of $\theta$ [Fig.~\ref{fig:KMnF3}(c)]. By fitting this $E(\theta)$ curve, we extract an effective Landau free energy for the antiferroaxial order. Finally, we demonstrate that epitaxial strain provides an efficient tuning knob for the antiferroaxial order: the distortion can be switched on or suppressed by strain, and the magnitude obeys an approximately linear relation $\mathbf G^2 \propto \theta^2 \sim \varepsilon$ [Fig.~\ref{fig:KMnF3}(d)], consistent with the strain-coupling analysis in Sec.~\ref{sec:3}. When $\varepsilon_{zz} < 94.5\%$, the angle $\theta=0$. Consequently, the spin splitting vanishes, and the system effectively reduces to a $T\tau$-invariant antiferromagnet.
L001178	
L001179	\textit{Computational details (KMnF$_3$):} We used $U=5$ eV on Mn, a $7\times 7\times 5$ k mesh, and a plane-wave cutoff energy of 550 eV.
L001180	
L001181	Ca$_2$MnO$_4$ crystallizes in the Ruddlesden-Popper perovskite structure~\cite{autretStructuralInvestigationCa2MnO42004}, and exhibits an analogous planar antiferroaxial rotation of MnO$_6$ octahedra around $\hat z$. This distortion lowers the symmetry from the parent space group $I4/mmm$ to isotropy subgroup $I4_1/acd$ and induces planar $g$-wave altermagnetic spin splitting. It exhibits an analogous planar antiferroaxial rotation of Mn-O$_6$ octahedra around $z$, as shown in Figs.~\ref{fig:Ca2MnO4}(a) and (b). Both antiferroaxial and antiferromagnetic phase transitions occur at the $P$ point with propagation vector $\mathbf{q}=(1/4,1/4,1/4)$, corresponding to the irreducible representations $G_z \sim P_2$ and $\mathbf{N} \sim P_1$, respectively. Upon condensation of $G_z$, the symmetry is lowered to the space group $I4_1/acd$. In this phase, the N\'{e}el vector transforms as $\mathbf{N} \sim \Gamma_2^+(A_{2g})$, which corresponds to a planar $g$-wave altermagnetism characterized by the $xy(x^2-y^2)$ symmetry [Table~\ref{table:altermagnet_valid_only}], as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:Ca2MnO4}(c).
L001182	
L001183	\textit{Computational details (Ca$_2$MnO$_4$):} We used $U=3$ eV on Mn and a $9\times 9\times 3$ k mesh (cutoff 550 eV).
