L001209	\subsection{3D bulk $g$-wave altermagnets: MF$_3$ (M = Fe, Co, Ni) and LaCrO$_3$}
L001210	\label{subsec:FeF3_family}
L001211	
L001212	FeF$_3$ [Fig.~\ref{fig:FeF3}] and its isostructural counterparts (CoF$_3$, NiF$_3$) host a bulk antiferroaxial distortion of corner-sharing M-F$_6$ octahedra~\cite{leeWeakferromagnetismCoF3FeF32018,mattssonDensityFunctionalTheory2019}. The high-temperature parent phase is $Pm\bar{3}m$. At low temperatures, both antiferroaxial and antiferromagnetic instabilities occur at the $R$ point $\mathbf{q}=(\pi,\pi,\pi)$.
L001213	Symmetry analysis gives $\mathbf{G}\sim R_4^+$, $\mathbf{N}\sim R_1^+$, so that the bilinear combination transforms as $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{N}\sim R_4^+\otimes R_1^+ = \Gamma_4^+(T_{1g})$. Since the rank four magnetic multipole contains the $T_{1g}$ spatial channel, a symmetry-allowed trilinear coupling is present, stabilizing a bulk $g$-wave altermagnetic state.
L001214	
L001215	The antiferroaxial magnitude is parameterized by the octahedral rotation angle $\theta$. The total-energy profile exhibits a double-well potential with degenerate minima at $\theta=\pm 12^\circ$ and a barrier of order $\Delta E\sim 30$ meV. The two antiferroaxial domains are related by a spin-group operation $[C_2||\boldsymbol{\tau}]$ with $\boldsymbol{\tau}=(0,0,1/2)$, enforcing a sign reversal of the nonrelativistic spin splitting upon $\theta\to-\theta$. With SOC included, the two domains are related by $\mathcal{T}\boldsymbol{\tau}$, implying that all time-reversal-odd response functions (e.g., anomalous Hall conductivity) must also reverse sign, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:FeF3}(d)
L001216	
L001217	For FeF$_3$, the anomalous Hall conductivity was evaluated using a Wannier-interpolated tight-binding model and the standard Kubo formula~\cite{wangInitioCalculationAnomalous2006,yaoFirstPrinciplesCalculation2004},
L001218	\begin{equation}
L001219	\sigma_{xy}=\frac{e^2}{\hbar}\int_\mathrm{BZ}\frac{d^3k}{(2\pi)^3}\sum_{m\neq n}(f_{m\mathbf{k}}-f_{n\mathbf{k}})
L001220	\frac{\mathrm{Im}\!\left[\langle \psi_{n\mathbf{k}}|v_x|\psi_{m\mathbf{k}}\rangle
L001221	\langle \psi_{m\mathbf{k}}|v_y|\psi_{n\mathbf{k}}\rangle\right]}
L001222	{(E_{m\mathbf{k}}-E_{n\mathbf{k}})^2},
L001223	\end{equation}
L001224	where $v_\alpha=\partial H_{\mathbf{k}}/\partial k_\alpha$ ($\alpha=x,y$), $f_{n\mathbf{k}}$ is the Fermi-Dirac distribution, and $E_{n\mathbf{k}}$ denotes the band energy.
L001225	
L001226	\textit{Computational details (FeF$_3$):} We used $U=5$ eV on Fe $3d$ orbitals and a plane-wave cutoff energy of 550 eV. The Brillouin-zone sampling was chosen consistently for a 3D bulk primitive cell.
L001227	
L001228	LaCrO$_3$ [Fig.~\ref{fig:LaCrO3}]~\cite{zhouMagneticStructure$mathrmLaCrO_3$2011} and CoF$_3$ [Fig.~\ref{fig:CoF3}] shares the same $R_4^+$-type octahedral rotation mode and therefore realizes the same bulk $g$-wave antiferroaxial altermagnetism [Fig.~\ref{fig:LaCrO3} and Fig.~\ref{fig:CoF3}]. For LaCrO$_3$ and CoF$_3$, we used $U=3$ eV for Cr in LaCrO$_3$ and $U=6$ eV for Co in CoF$_3$, respectively (cutoff 550 eV).
